update lr_explanation_choice_notes t set body=v.nb from (values
('f0700707-34f8-4675-a947-04f260f9f566'::uuid,'6dbe95597a67ae6cf753b433f29e4359','A is about differences among the managers, but the argument compares each manager''s normal coffee intake with extra coffee. Negate it: the managers did not differ in both abilities; say they were all alike. The conclusion is untouched, since what extra coffee does to overall ability is the same question either way.'),
('1782ac5b-3845-44f6-883a-61745db406d6'::uuid,'1f2f9ad762dc0d312632fe60d4eeca7e','B compares drinking less coffee with drinking more, and the argument never considers drinking less. Negate it: drinking less coffee would hurt management ability just as much as drinking more. Extra coffee could still impair overall ability, so the argument does not need B.'),
('3bf1ad8e-18a0-41ab-a453-25b0a8996104'::uuid,'32a3df6f2bdf046a916c853c1bd1116b','C is tempting because it points at other factors, the very gap we found. But it ranks how important those factors are, and the gap is about what coffee does to them. Negate C: some other factor matters more than speed or integration. That alone does not hurt the argument, because extra coffee might leave that factor untouched. We need an answer about coffee''s effect, not a ranking.'),
('f13dec0c-a973-45af-b396-ee61f1861b15'::uuid,'b088a47db149aff47487544818057bac','D rules out our objection. Negate it: in the experiment, drinking more coffee did have beneficial effects on overall management ability that outweighed the drop in integration. Now the conclusion that extra coffee impairs overall ability is flatly contradicted.

When an argument moves from a few measured effects to a claim about the overall result, look for an answer saying no unmeasured benefit cancels the measured harm. Notice the modest wording, too. D does not say coffee had no benefits at all, only none that outweighed the loss. A necessary assumption usually claims no more than the argument needs.'),
('ad514253-f918-4dcc-bd7a-fa7dfe74c2b1'::uuid,'6741d84214ff97e685e50fca7fa26e31','E says coffee is the most important factor in processing speed. The argument needs only what the experiment showed: extra coffee raised speed in these managers. Negate E: other factors matter more for speed. The experimental result still stands, and so does the argument.'),
('b5eb2aec-a2d9-4c59-9947-d9913a3dd8dd'::uuid,'64fbfdc54ec0f4fa45adad733dabf4db','A is about deception, and the argument is about consistency. A narrator could mislead the reader while staying consistent with everything else in the novel, so deception is not the trait in question. Negate A: some narrators are deceptive. That says nothing about whether narrators introduce inconsistent information, so the argument survives.'),
('41e60f3d-ace8-407d-8244-8b3d8c8a4e03'::uuid,'919f5df76f7e6db06ab302c37552e6da','B says a narrator''s voice is sometimes necessary in plays that employ a chorus. The argument compares a chorus with a novel''s narrator; it never asks what a play needs. Negate B: plays with a chorus never need a narrator''s voice. The difference between chorus and narrator is untouched.'),
('da835237-aab3-43ce-a2c3-771c813a1d16'::uuid,'eaaf1f4ab2e60afaee1719c45e5ccb00','C adds another trait of the chorus: it sometimes introduces information the audience needs. But the premise already gave us the chorus''s side. The missing piece is on the narrator''s side. Negate C: the chorus never introduces necessary information. Its inconsistency still stands, and so does the argument.'),
('b3eb8b7b-d986-47c0-8a44-c837d7f7f959'::uuid,'8cb476c43747213f57f73fc63fd1bcd2','D supplies the missing half of the comparison. The premise says the chorus sometimes introduces inconsistent information; D says a narrator never does. Negate it: information introduced by a narrator can sometimes be inconsistent with the rest of the novel. Now inconsistency is something chorus and narrator share, and it can no longer prove they differ.

Whenever an argument says two things differ because one of them has a trait, check the other side. The answer often says the second thing lacks that trait.'),
('e7732893-26b2-4393-8d55-d339e5101bef'::uuid,'a3ece2361a25e1476a49cb3d0f66bd87','E is tempting because it sounds like the inconsistency premise. But it talks about choruses again, and the premise already covers them. Negate E: authors never use choruses to mislead. A chorus''s information could still be inconsistent for some other reason, so the difference stands. The argument needs a claim about narrators, not more about choruses.'),
('0d179acb-747a-4331-87dc-c7693e449427'::uuid,'7af60acd85bee0b3fdb5cfd17571eb0f','A says human habitation will be established on Mars only if transport costs decrease. The argument is about economic feasibility, not about whether habitation will ever happen. Negate A: habitation could be established without a drop in transport costs, perhaps by a government willing to pay any price. Colonizing could still be economically infeasible, so the argument survives.'),
('8571e1fe-e6a5-4949-99ca-1141c159475b'::uuid,'43a5c588b79ee0a3e7c8fd93f2fa58b3','B is tempting because a future price drop sounds like an objection. Negate it: transport costs are expected to decrease in the near future. Decrease by how much? A cost that falls from astronomical to merely enormous is still too high, and the premise says the costs "would be so high." The argument survives a modest decrease, so it does not need B.'),
('9c79500e-b8de-42cd-aac9-3f40835ccb4e'::uuid,'9c00a46cabd6b37f3246df531d3f5557','C is in the right territory, since it rules out other sources of materials. But it rules out too much. Negate C: Earth is not the only source. Maybe some materials could come from an asteroid, which still means hauling them through space at great cost. That source would not make the colony affordable, so the argument survives. The argument needs only that Mars itself cannot practically supply the materials.'),
('499051ad-9656-4321-b8ec-30781ead655c'::uuid,'3ed5a650c6e47bca69d85deca4091f0f','D says no significant benefit would result from habitation on Mars. Economic feasibility weighs benefits against costs, so the argument needs only that the benefits fall short of the enormous cost. Negate D: some significant benefit would result. A significant benefit can still be smaller than the cost, so the argument survives.'),
('31a98f97-f0fe-4f3a-8934-f0c23ef7ef4a'::uuid,'ae3b8d7e3a0890a43bb8cffb146693ac','E shuts down our first objection. Negate it: Mars is a practical source of the basic materials. Then the colony could assemble its materials on site, the shipping bill shrinks, and the premise about transport costs no longer supports the conclusion. The argument quietly assumed the materials have to make the trip.

On a practical argument like this one, ask how the plan could still work, then look for the answer that closes that route. E is also modestly worded: Mars is not a "practical source," which is weaker than claiming Mars has no materials at all. That is why it beats C.'),
('03b432cf-7013-4934-9d05-1755544b9320'::uuid,'e7ddef9e89705bcfe651e21b3ea8bbde','A is about stars in two different galaxies and whether we can tell their distances. The argument studies stars within one galaxy, and it never claims that method is the only option. Negate A: we can tell whether stars in different galaxies are about the same distance from Earth. Studying stars in one galaxy still works, so the argument survives.'),
('f000c431-4adc-46ea-b2d9-521ecdc15a76'::uuid,'cf444317e8fa16a4bca3c37585430b44','B is close in spirit, since the elements a star burns might be one of the "other characteristics." But it is far too strong: for any two stars in the same distant galaxy, differences in their elements will be detectable. Negate it: for some pair, a difference in elements is not detectable. The study could still use other characteristics, or other pairs, so the argument survives. The argument needs some observable characteristic, not this one for every pair.'),
('8aff2e25-ed1c-4803-bdb3-7e4bf6755d04'::uuid,'8f814b8de5ea8159ef5a99f38c006639','C is about our own galaxy. The argument is only about distant galaxies, where the distances between stars are tiny compared with the distance to the galaxy. Negate C: the stars in our galaxy are all about the same distance from Earth. Nothing about the distant-galaxy study changes.'),
('fccec791-9884-4917-9e30-080a2a6dd3ab'::uuid,'87642b372cb01056b61c17d0754f2ace','D gives move two what it needs: something besides brightness that we can actually see. Negate it: no star in any distant galaxy has a characteristic, other than brightness, discernible from Earth. Then the study has brightness figures and nothing to correlate them with, and the conclusion fails.

When a conclusion says we can find a correlation, check that both things being correlated can actually be observed. Notice how modest D is: it says only that there "are stars" with such characteristics. A necessary assumption often states just the minimum the plan cannot do without.'),
('57b26ce6-355e-433a-8ca9-b8fc9eab34c1'::uuid,'fce4a84f4424737bf44e33653e97e08b','E says a big difference in distance produces a big difference in apparent brightness. The argument relies on the opposite situation: within one distant galaxy, distances are nearly the same, so distance cannot explain the brightness gaps. Negate E: two stars at very different distances might still look equally bright. That says nothing about stars at nearly the same distance, so the argument survives.'),
('0e2e7822-a1bc-49f7-a7c1-ca2959f1bf15'::uuid,'923aac092b9d3e737c9a4570436574d7','A is the link between no natural water and domestication. Negate it: even without domestication, bovines are likely to exist in a region with no natural sources of water. Then the bovines'' presence in this dry region proves nothing about people keeping them, and the conclusion falls.

Notice the soft wording, "unlikely to exist." The argument does not need wild bovines to be impossible there, only improbable. When an argument moves from an odd fact to the one explanation it favors, the answer often says the rival explanation would not happen.'),
('bddf34e2-a2e5-4efd-87a1-e15d78c39ef4'::uuid,'da28de064292f42ab83ae0a463c50834','B says domesticating animals is something a society must adopt early to move to agriculture. The argument runs the other way: these people domesticated bovines, so they were not exclusively hunter-gatherers. Negate B: some societies move to agriculture without domesticating animals first. These people still domesticated bovines, so the conclusion stands.'),
('4a397b82-bdc0-4b91-933d-2cc10fd6d22a'::uuid,'4e77f5f4300da47c0d43baea7d700051','C says people could have helped other large mammals live in the region. The argument is about why the bovines were there, not about what else people might have done. Negate C: even with human help, other large mammals could not have lived there. The bovines were still there without natural water, so the argument is untouched.'),
('44cdfd6e-03ed-4ddd-9ba7-f42ffb1663ff'::uuid,'46ea4f55e28e2bf58f41a653a8cc491f','D is too strong. The conclusion says the people were "no longer exclusively hunter-gatherers," which leaves room for them to keep hunting while also herding. Negate D: some cultures get food both ways. That fits the conclusion perfectly, so the argument does not need D.'),
('7c4077a6-5d08-431e-ad1a-5f5379e8f3df'::uuid,'fce792f851f957d3194702f649ed5fad','E compares the water needs of domesticated and wild animals. The argument does not need domesticated bovines to drink less. It needs wild bovines to be unlikely where there is no natural water. Negate E: domesticated animals need as much water as wild ones. People could still be bringing that water, so the argument survives.'),
('7d84100c-80c4-43e1-bc44-ff2c8ec3860b'::uuid,'11619afd9a3176d5d8c054e9d5749e55','A adds a requirement the argument never mentions: patients focusing on other people''s troubles. The argument is about the patient''s own relationships. Negate A: therapists can help change relationships even if patients do not focus on other people''s troubles. That fits the psychotherapist''s plan fine, so the argument survives.'),
('e4d05c13-b86a-4864-b263-f4266815e4d7'::uuid,'af806bf6de4f8b62419f8233648c4aa1','B says some patients cannot be healed if the therapist helps them change their relationships. That cuts against the psychotherapist, who recommends exactly that focus. Negate B: every patient can be healed even when the therapist helps them change their relationships. Nothing in the argument is hurt by that, so B is not required.'),
('b67988ae-1e3d-4acb-afad-fbffc64d9a30'::uuid,'e19a4dbf3c85d9a1f0326b1b85a065e6','C is tempting because it says changing relationships brings relief, which sounds like the psychotherapist''s view. But the conclusion is a "must": relationship work is required, not guaranteed to work. Negate C: some patients who change their relationships get no relief. Relationship work could still be required for healing, so the argument survives. C also covers any change, while the psychotherapist calls for positive change.'),
('28a1ff5c-7464-4313-8318-d58d89134328'::uuid,'e6aec33ffb5ec8578d126aa64d7de567','D says the internal-only route can''t work, which is exactly what the "must" requires. Negate it: some psychotherapist can help a patient heal solely by addressing the internal causes. Then the therapist does not have to focus on relationships, and the conclusion collapses.

On a "to get X, you must do Y" conclusion, look for the answer that closes off the alternatives to Y. Here the obvious alternative is treating the internal causes alone, and D rules it out.'),
('8db59f5c-2b7f-4f67-a145-5d923bdc865c'::uuid,'6453776318f3880c3801c777d4c04120','E says that helping patients focus on purely internal troubles provides the relief they seek. That points away from the psychotherapist, who says the troubles "do not have purely internal causes." The argument needs internal-only work to fall short, not to succeed. Negate E: such a focus does not always bring relief. That fits the argument fine, so E is not required.'),
('60f192ba-c097-4e6a-a606-41c34c2e1079'::uuid,'0ca9c1594980e1837a2621ed352d8e93','A gives a reason the ratings might drop: shows sold early are aging hits about to decline. The argument already has the ratings drop from the study. What it lacks is the step from ratings to revenue. Negate A: shows sold early are not likely to decline soon. The study still shows the drop, so the argument survives.'),
('3a8b6088-8ad9-44f9-acda-00b689b5fb49'::uuid,'e7271c46dbd9da69b7f72016b3ed1b13','B is the missing link. The evidence stops at ratings; the conclusion is about revenue. Negate it: a drop in ratings has no negative effect on the network''s revenues. Then the study''s ratings drop tells us nothing about revenue, and the conclusion has no support.

Whenever a new term shows up in the conclusion, here "revenues," look for the answer that connects it to what the evidence actually talks about.'),
('57c331d4-9250-48d5-ad38-ca10a158a894'::uuid,'2b176df6ab73f4bab4821f023f77bc3a','C, if anything, works against the argument: compensation built into the syndication price would offset the network''s losses. Negate C: the price includes no compensation for the network. That leaves the network''s loss in place, which fits the argument. The argument does not need C.'),
('7b55b004-4990-4f07-a846-5070fd9f6ab5'::uuid,'eb6dc908ae712845ee5ede93ea6fd6a1','D says the audience usually prefers first-run episodes to reruns. If anything, that makes the drop harder to explain, since fans who prefer new episodes would keep watching them. The argument doesn''t need to explain the drop; the study already shows it happened. Negate D: the audience does not usually prefer first-run episodes. The drop is just as real, so the argument survives.'),
('a2ba65f8-0bb7-480f-9dd8-543190d653f7'::uuid,'171997ec341c6a2679a346db4404ac9d','E is about how common syndication is. The argument concerns only programs sold into syndication while still running. Negate E: at least half of programs are sold into syndication at some point. That changes nothing about whether a ratings drop costs the network money.'),
('66a5302c-409d-450e-8c65-e3191c6852aa'::uuid,'8943f2d636ff5d0982fbe38a37d1d1e4','A says the medium would not adequately explain the low visibility even if it existed. The professor never needs to deny that the medium could explain it; the point is that relativity already does. Negate A: the medium would adequately explain the low visibility. The argument is untouched, since two theories can each explain the same thing.'),
('3e1addcb-06a2-42d9-b5b1-69954dd40989'::uuid,'3996cf6d19c4d6ec1e14ff88c7e01add','B says the hypothesis is correct only if it adequately explains the low visibility. That sets a requirement for the medium hypothesis to be true, and the professor never relies on one. Negate B: the hypothesis could be correct without explaining the low visibility. The professor can still say the low visibility gives no reason to believe it, so the argument survives.'),
('ee54acaa-1645-4de6-a567-00d17a063ed4'::uuid,'7d1a85a729f7f84eea587b6e5f2e9c0d','C sounds like the professor''s principle, but it covers a different case. It says a hypothesis is likely correct when it explains something an existing theory does not adequately account for. Here relativity does account for the low visibility, so C never applies. Negate C: such a hypothesis is not necessarily likely to be correct. The professor''s argument doesn''t care, because it is about the case where an existing theory already explains everything.'),
('553a1b8f-2ae5-404a-a629-3047dfd4bcd1'::uuid,'5343005265cfdb001ab50d507791d4c9','D is about what most of the scientists believe. The argument turns on whether relativity explains the low visibility, not on who accepts relativity. Negate D: most of these scientists reject relativity. Relativity''s explanation is just as complete either way, so the argument survives.'),
('87a139de-74be-4c0e-b4cf-b05a1c3e58a6'::uuid,'0ee115baec52c51a19bdd8445bdcdc39','E rules out the overlap objection. Negate it: the general theory of relativity does depend on the hypothesis that the medium exists. Then relativity''s explanation of the low visibility is itself a reason to believe in the medium, and the professor''s conclusion falls apart.

When an argument dismisses one explanation because another already covers the facts, check that the second explanation does not quietly rely on the first. That is the defender this kind of argument needs.')
) v(id,ob,nb) where t.id=v.id and md5(t.body)=v.ob returning t.id;